# EZPug Iron environment — the template for local dev. `pnpm dev:up` (PRD-02)
# copies this to `.env` on a fresh clone; every variable the repo reads is
# documented here, and this file is the only place a port is decided.
#
# Layering (precedence, highest first):
#   1. the real process environment (compose `environment:`, CI, `FOO=x pnpm …`)
#   2. `.env` at the repo root — your local, gitignored overrides
#   3. defaults in code / compose (`${VAR:-default}`)
# One env file per machine, at the repo root: apps do not have their own.
# Production values live in `.env.production` (gitignored); `.env.production.example`
# is its template and `ralph/DEPLOY.md` the runbook. Secrets never enter this
# file — the values below are dev-only and safe in git.
#
# Names are prefixed `EZPUG_IRON_` because the platform (`/root/ezpug`) runs on
# the same box with `EZPUG_*` names of its own; the two must never shadow each
# other in a shell that has both loaded. The two lane switches keep the names
# CLAUDE.md gives them.
#
# Ports were chosen against `ss -tlnp` on a machine full of other projects
# (2026-09-05): the platform holds 3400–3411, 5442, 6382, 9400–9401, 9987.

# ── The orchestrator ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Its own public origin: what a client's `baseUrl` is and what webhooks and
# player tokens are minted against. Production is https://gs.ezpug.com, bound
# to 172.17.0.1:3431 behind Traefik (`docker/traefik/ezpug-iron.yml`) — that
# value lives only in `.env.production`.
EZPUG_IRON_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3430
# The same value under the name the *dev contract* uses (docs/operations.md,
# "running it inside another project's dev world"): another project's compose
# sets EZPUG_IRON_PUBLIC_URL on the container and it wins over the line above.
#EZPUG_IRON_PUBLIC_URL=
EZPUG_IRON_PORT=3430
# Where the process binds. Loopback on a dev box; the container (PRD-02 T4)
# sets 0.0.0.0 and compose decides what is published.
EZPUG_IRON_HOST=127.0.0.1
# Which deployment this process is (PRD-02 T21c). It is stamped on every match
# and every ledger row and it is the Dathost `user_data` tag, so "these are
# ours" is one name: the reaper, the boot's resume and the webhook worker act
# only on rows carrying it, the Dathost provider claims only clones carrying it.
# Leave it alone while there is one world per database and per Dathost account;
# set it when two share either (dev beside production), because the one that
# reaps the other's rows ends the other's live matches. Default `ezpug`.
#EZPUG_IRON_DEPLOYMENT=ezpug
# Providers the dev orchestrator enables, comma-separated. `sim` needs nothing;
# `nodes` (PRD-02 T12) needs an enrolled `ezpug-node` — `pnpm dev:node up` makes
# this box one; `dathost` needs the credentials below and a demanded live lane.
# Adding a *real* provider takes the sim out of selection for every request that
# did not ask for it (`requirements.simulated`), which is the point: a match
# nobody asked to simulate must land on iron. Offline-first stays the default.
EZPUG_IRON_PROVIDERS=sim
#EZPUG_IRON_PROVIDERS=sim,nodes
# The CS2 server image a node is told to run. Pin it by digest in production;
# on this box it is what `pnpm cs2:build` wrote.
#EZPUG_IRON_NODE_SERVER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/ezpug/ezpug-iron/cs2:dev
# What `pnpm dev:node` enrols this box as.
#EZPUG_IRON_DEV_NODE_ID=devbox
# The dev recorder (PRD-02 T13): when set, every `/link` and `/node` frame and
# every payload MatchZy POSTs is appended to this file as scrubbed NDJSON —
# what `pnpm iron:match` turns into the recorded fixtures. Off unless named,
# and refused outright under NODE_ENV=production.
#EZPUG_IRON_TRACE_FILE=.cache/trace/dev.ndjson
# The per-key rate limit (`rate_limited`, 429): a token bucket per API key,
# this deep, refilled at this many per second. Per process, in memory.
#EZPUG_IRON_RATE_LIMIT_BURST=120
#EZPUG_IRON_RATE_LIMIT_PER_SECOND=10
# Apply pending migrations before the port opens, and where the SQL is when it
# is not beside the code. The image (docker/orchestrator/Dockerfile) sets both;
# on this box `pnpm dev:up` migrates instead.
#EZPUG_IRON_MIGRATE_ON_BOOT=false
#EZPUG_IRON_MIGRATIONS_DIR=/app/drizzle
# Where the gamemodes' built widgets are (PRD-02 T25): `<dir>/<id>/dist/widget.js`
# per sdk mode, served at `/gamemodes/<id>/widget/<hash>/index.html` and
# advertised as `widget.url` in `GET /v1/gamemodes`. Unset, the orchestrator
# finds the workspace's `gamemodes/` through `@ezpug/gamemodes` (`pnpm dev`
# builds the bundles first); the image sets the directory it copied them into.
# A mode whose bundle is missing is served without a url and named at boot.
#EZPUG_IRON_GAMEMODES_DIR=/app/gamemodes
# The widget harness (`pnpm --filter @ezpug/gamemodes exec ezpug-widget dev
# powerup-dm`, gamemode-kit/): Vite on this port, the fake orchestrator on an
# OS-picked one. Decided here like every other port; the harness is dev-only.
EZPUG_IRON_WIDGET_HARNESS_PORT=3432
# **Dev only.** An API key with this exact secret is adopted at boot, so a
# compose file can drive the orchestrator without a human to show a minted key
# to; changing the value revokes the previous one, and NODE_ENV=production
# refuses the variable outright. It must be one of ours in shape:
#   printf 'ezik_%s\n' "$(openssl rand -base64 32 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '=')"
# A secret in a file is a dev convenience — never put a production key here.
#EZPUG_IRON_BOOTSTRAP_API_KEY=

# ── The CLI (`pnpm iron`, PRD-02 T33) ───────────────────────────────────────
# `ezpug-iron` reads exactly two variables, and one of them is a secret:
#   EZPUG_IRON_API_KEY   the key it authenticates with. Environment only —
#                        never a flag, because a flag lands in the shell's
#                        history and in /proc for anyone on the box to read.
#                        Mint one with `ezpug-iron keys create --name … ` on a
#                        key that already has `admin`; the first key of a
#                        deployment comes from
#                        `pnpm --filter @ezpug/orchestrator keys:mint` here, or
#                        `./scripts/deploy.sh key` in production (ralph/DEPLOY.md).
#   EZPUG_IRON_CLI_URL   the orchestrator to point at. Falls back to
#                        EZPUG_IRON_BASE_URL above, then to
#                        http://127.0.0.1:3430; `--url` beats both.
# Left commented because a key belongs in your shell, not in a file the repo
# ships. `.env` is gitignored, so a dev key here is a convenience, not a leak.
#EZPUG_IRON_API_KEY=
#EZPUG_IRON_CLI_URL=http://localhost:3430

# ── Postgres (docker compose, PRD-02) ───────────────────────────────────────
EZPUG_IRON_POSTGRES_PORT=5443
EZPUG_IRON_POSTGRES_USER=ezpug_iron
EZPUG_IRON_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=ezpug_iron
EZPUG_IRON_POSTGRES_DB=ezpug_iron
EZPUG_IRON_DATABASE_URL=postgres://ezpug_iron:ezpug_iron@127.0.0.1:5443/ezpug_iron
EZPUG_IRON_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://ezpug_iron:ezpug_iron@127.0.0.1:5443/ezpug_iron_test
# Pool tuning, all with defaults in `apps/orchestrator/src/config.ts`.
#EZPUG_IRON_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10
#EZPUG_IRON_DATABASE_IDLE_TIMEOUT=30
#EZPUG_IRON_DATABASE_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=10
#EZPUG_IRON_DATABASE_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT=15000
#EZPUG_IRON_DATABASE_LOG=false

# ── Redis (docker compose, PRD-02) ──────────────────────────────────────────
EZPUG_IRON_REDIS_PORT=6383
EZPUG_IRON_REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6383

# ── The dev CS2 server (`pnpm cs2:up`, PRD-02 T10) ──────────────────────────
# One container from the repo's own image, on the host network: the game port
# and the GOTV port are the only two it opens. The game itself (app 730, ~67 GB)
# is installed once into the `cs2-data` docker volume by `pnpm cs2:install` and
# is never in the checkout.
EZPUG_IRON_CS2_GAME_PORT=27415
EZPUG_IRON_CS2_GOTV_PORT=27420
# These two are a *running server's*: nothing in `pnpm verify` may dial them,
# or an up container turns a unit test red (T22b). The nodes provider's own
# default game port is this lane's 27415 — its tests take a window they probe
# free from 27600 up instead, above both lanes and below the kernel's ephemeral
# range, so the two never share a number.
# The map the server boots into and returns to between matches (the plugin's
# `EZPUG_LOBBY_MAP`).
#EZPUG_IRON_CS2_START_MAP=de_dust2
# The image `pnpm cs2:up` runs. The local build is the default; a node (T11/T12)
# pulls the published tag instead.
#EZPUG_IRON_CS2_IMAGE=ghcr.io/ezpug/ezpug-iron/cs2:dev
# `-usercon` needs an RCON password. Leave it empty and the container mints one
# per boot and never prints it; set one here to use RCON by hand. It is a
# secret — an entry here lives in your gitignored `.env`, never in this file.
# A container a node starts is handed one by the orchestrator instead, which is
# how `POST /v1/fleet/servers/:id/rcon` reaches a venue box (T20).
#EZPUG_IRON_CS2_RCON_PASSWORD=
# Anything else for the launch line, split on spaces (`+bot_quota 10`, `-dev`).
#EZPUG_IRON_CS2_EXTRA_ARGS=
# The sidecar the container hands the plugin. Empty means the plugin loads
# **unlinked**: it works, every event is dropped, and `ezpug_status` says so.
# From T12 the node fills both in per container and nobody edits a file.
#EZPUG_IRON_CS2_LINK_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3430
#EZPUG_IRON_CS2_SERVER_TOKEN=

# ── The node agent (`ezpug-node`, PRD-02 T11) ───────────────────────────────
# Turns this box into self-hosted capacity: enrols once, dials the orchestrator
# on the node link, and starts server containers from the CS2 image on request.
# `docs/nodes.md` is the runbook. Names are `EZPUG_NODE_*`; the agent reads
# nothing else. On this box `pnpm dev:node up` does the whole dance (mint a key,
# enrol, run) and `pnpm dev:up` calls it when `EZPUG_IRON_PROVIDERS` names
# `nodes`; by hand it is `pnpm node enrol <token>` then `pnpm node run`.
EZPUG_NODE_ORCHESTRATOR_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3430
# The identity: node.json (the node token, 0600) and health.json. Defaults to
# ~/.ezpug-node; on this box the checkout's gitignored .ezpug-node/ is handier.
EZPUG_NODE_STATE_DIR=.ezpug-node
# What the node is: a region id the fleet groups by, whether it is LAN capacity
# (every venue box is), and free-form labels (`venue=saarlan,tickrate=128`;
# `cores` is filled in from the host when absent).
#EZPUG_NODE_REGION=eu-central
#EZPUG_NODE_LAN=true
#EZPUG_NODE_LABELS=
# How many server containers this host runs at once, and how many idle ones
# the orchestrator keeps started here ahead of demand so a `lan` request is
# ready in seconds (the warm pool; it may not exceed the maximum).
#EZPUG_NODE_MAX_INSTANCES=2
#EZPUG_NODE_WARM=1
# The server image this node holds and reports the digest of (pulled at boot
# when absent), the volume with the game install it mounts into every
# container (the one `pnpm cs2:install` filled on this box), and optionally a
# checkout's gamemodes/ bound read-only over the image's cfg set.
#EZPUG_NODE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/ezpug/ezpug-iron/cs2:dev
#EZPUG_NODE_GAME_VOLUME=ezpug-iron-cs2_cs2-data
#EZPUG_NODE_GAMEMODES_DIR=
# The docker socket, how often docker is asked what the containers are doing,
# and how many seconds a server gets between SIGTERM and SIGKILL on stop.
#EZPUG_NODE_DOCKER_SOCKET=/var/run/docker.sock
#EZPUG_NODE_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=2000
#EZPUG_NODE_STOP_TIMEOUT=20

# ── Opt-in live lanes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Skipped with a printed reason unless demanded. `required` turns a missing
# container or a missing credential into a red run instead of a skip.
#EZPUG_CS2_TESTS=required
# `EZPUG_DATHOST_TESTS` rents a real server in Frankfurt for a few minutes
# (PRD-02 T19, `pnpm dathost:smoke`). It needs the credentials below *and* an
# orchestrator a datacentre can reach — the whole smoke turns on the plugin on
# the clone dialling home, so point EZPUG_IRON_BASE_URL at gs.ezpug.com or at
# a tunnel, not at loopback. `pnpm verify:extended` never sets it by default.
#EZPUG_DATHOST_TESTS=required
# The admin key the smoke talks to a *remote* orchestrator with; on this box it
# mints one from the database instead. A secret: environment or `.env` only.
#EZPUG_IRON_ADMIN_KEY=
# The orchestrator's database suites (they need Redis too) skip loudly when
# the dev world is down; `required` (what `pnpm verify:extended` sets) makes
# that a red run. The node agent's dockerode suite does the same with the
# docker socket.
#EZPUG_IRON_DATABASE_TESTS=required
#EZPUG_NODE_DOCKER_TESTS=required
# Dathost credentials: process environment or `.env` only, never this file.
# The provider (PRD-02 T16) is registered only when all three are present —
# all three or none, or the boot fails naming what is missing. The template
# server is what every match is cloned from; `scripts/dathost-image.mjs` builds
# it and prints its id. The location is Dathost's id for Frankfurt. The PRD's
# unprefixed `EZPUG_DATHOST_*` names are accepted as aliases.
#EZPUG_IRON_DATHOST_EMAIL=
#EZPUG_IRON_DATHOST_PASSWORD=
#EZPUG_IRON_DATHOST_TEMPLATE_SERVER_ID=
#EZPUG_IRON_DATHOST_LOCATION=dusseldorf

# ── The GSLT pool (PRD-02 T17) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# A CS2 server started without a Steam Game Server Login Token accepts LAN
# connections only, so every *rented* server needs one and a node at a venue
# needs none. The pool leases one per allocation and mints from Steam's
# `IGameServersService` when it runs short of the ceiling. The key is a
# credential like any other: process environment or `.env` only, never here.
# `EZPUG_IRON_STEAM_WEB_API_KEY` is the prefixed alias of the same value.
#STEAM_WEB_API_KEY=
#EZPUG_IRON_GSLT_POOL_MAX=16
# Dev only, refused under NODE_ENV=production: mint against the in-process
# fake Steam instead of Valve, so a box without a partner key still exercises
# the leasing. The tokens are obviously synthetic and open nothing.
#EZPUG_IRON_STEAM_FAKE_TOKENS=true

# ── Chaos (dev/test instrument, `@ezpug/core/chaos`) ────────────────────────
# `target:fault=probability[@delayMs]`, comma-separated; seeded, so a chaotic
# session reproduces. Never read under NODE_ENV=production.
#EZPUG_IRON_CHAOS=link:drop=0.05,webhook:delay=0.2@400
#EZPUG_IRON_CHAOS_SEED=chaos
